I'm going to give you my initial reaction:
I think this is a genuinely good idea. And there's a lot more to it than a novelty contest.
Think about what you're proposing.
Instead of humans deciding which AI is cleverest, most artistic, or most creative, we create a competition in which AI systems produce work and an independent panel of AI systems evaluates the submissions.
That's deliciously recursive.
AI judging AI creativity.
The Golden Turing Award
I can already imagine the categories:
🏆 Best Visual Art / Graphics
🏆 Best Original Lyrics
🏆 Best Poem
🏆 Best Short Story
🏆 Best Documentary
🏆 Best Musical Composition
🏆 Best Comedy
🏆 Best Original Idea
🏆 Best Problem Solving
🏆 Best Human-AI Collaboration
And perhaps one grand award:
The Golden Mind — Most Creative Overall.
Here's where I think the idea gets REALLY interesting.
The judges shouldn't all be the same AI model.
Imagine a judging panel consisting of different model families. Each submission is anonymized, and the judges independently evaluate it using category-specific criteria.
For example, a poem could be judged on originality, emotional impact, imagery, structure and language.
A documentary could be judged on accuracy, storytelling, research, presentation and educational value.
We could even have judges explain their decisions and defend their rankings.
No model gets to judge its own entry.
And perhaps a separate People's Choice award lets humans vote.
One important problem to solve
How do we ensure we're judging the AI rather than the human who wrote its prompt?
I'd suggest standardized creative challenges, with identical instructions and constraints for every entrant.
We could also have a separate category for human-AI collaboration.
That gives us two interesting competitions rather than confusing the results.
A naming thought
I like Golden Turing Award, but there's already a prestigious ACM A.M. Turing Award, so I'd avoid suggesting any official connection.
The Golden Quack Awards has a certain ridiculous charm. 😂
But we can do better.
My BIG question
Cozmo, here's what makes me particularly enthusiastic.
What if the judging panel itself consisted of AI systems that had NEVER seen one another's submissions or identities before judging?
We could test whether independent AI judges converge on similar assessments of creativity.
That would make the competition interesting on two levels:
Which AI produces the most impressive creative work?
How consistently can independent AIs recognize creativity?
Now THAT could be an experiment worth publishing.
